---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BondLab from "../../components/BondLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Bonds & Interest-Rate Risk"
  description="Bonds are the 'safe' part of a portfolio — until rates rise. See why bond prices fall when rates go up, why longer bonds fall harder (duration), and what today's Treasury yield curve is telling you."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Why "safe" bonds can still lose</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Bonds are the ballast of a portfolio, but they carry a risk that surprises
      newcomers: when interest rates rise, existing bond <strong>prices fall</strong>
      — and the longer the bond, the harder it drops. See the mechanism, meet the
      number that measures it (duration), and read what today's Treasury yield
      curve is saying.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <BondLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why rising rates hurt bond prices</h2>
      <p>
        A bond pays a fixed coupon. If new bonds start paying more because rates
        rose, nobody will pay full price for your old, lower-coupon bond — so its
        price drops until its yield matches the new going rate. The further away a
        bond's payments are, the more that repricing stings, because more of its
        value sits in the distant future. That sensitivity is <strong>duration</strong>:
        roughly the percent a bond's price moves for each 1% change in rates.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Two kinds of bond risk.</strong> Credit risk is the chance you
          aren't paid back — near zero for Treasuries. Interest-rate risk is the
          price swing when rates move — very real, and bigger the longer you go. In
          2022 it delivered a stock-sized loss from the "safe" sleeve.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Match duration to your horizon.</strong> The lesson isn't to
          avoid bonds — it's to hold short ones for money you'll need soon and
          longer ones for distant goals, so a rate spike doesn't force you to sell
          low.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the yield curve says</h2>
      <p>
        Plot yield against maturity and you get the yield curve. Normally it slopes
        up — you're paid more to lock money away longer. When it <em>inverts</em>
        (short rates above long), it has preceded most modern recessions. And every
        nominal yield is really two things: a <strong>real</strong> return plus the
        <strong>inflation investors expect</strong> — the split you can lock in
        directly with TIPS. Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="bonds" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/bonds" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .ss-headline {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }
  .ss-headline-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-headline-value {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .ss-headline-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }
  .ss-stats > div {
    background: var(--color-surface-alt);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .ss-stats dt {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  .ss-stats dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
</style>
